this great big story was made possible  by she'll make the future as an engineer  I'm always watching how things swim and  sort of thinking about them the goal is  to really be inspired by nature and take  this system that Nature has created and  understand the physics and then build  that capability into a new vehicle  [Music]  I'm Megan Leftwich associate professor  of mechanical engineering at the George  Washington University nice study sea  lion most animals swim with their tails  or their fluke or whatever's the back of  their body but sea lions use their four  flippers and clap them into their body  to generate a thrust that can propel  them through the water at incredible  speeds this form of swimming is found in  almost no other swimming animal if we  could understand how sea lions move we  could design vehicles that could one day  stealthily explore shipwrecks underwater  mines or unexplored caves  in 2013 I started to get serious about  studying sea lions and I put together a  team of students and other researchers  and keepers at the Smithsonian National  Zoo so I thought well we could use our  modern techniques and really get into  the nitty-gritty of the physics of how  these animals swim so we 3d print three  pieces with joints that represent the  elbow joint and the wrist joint and we  use different silicon gels to create a  soft robotic flipper and then what we're  able to do is use motors to actually  actuate that so we can recreate that  motion that we got from filming the  animals at the zoo directly from the  animals we then put that on to this  robotic platform to study not only how  the animals move but how the animals  move the water it's an understanding the  physics of that can allow us to then  build some of those capabilities into  the engineered vehicles that we have  it's important to take notes from nature  when inventing technology because Nature  has already done so much of the work for  us and so by looking around we can  definitely broaden our horizons and see  solutions that already exist we're  taking nature which seems to solve  problems so elegantly and being inspired  by those elegant solutions and you know  we often say like nature does that so  much better than us and then move on but  maybe if we say well how do they do it  we could start to increase our own  functionality  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

